    "product_id": "087cc7ef-6ce7-465a-9a39-14e8bd9d70f6",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 221141\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1231 AM PST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Breezy periods at times this week but widespread wind impacts are \n  not anticipated.\n\n* Warming trend expected through the week as below normal\n  temperature today climb to above normal by Friday. Temperatures\n  will remain above normal despite the potential for another weak\n  system this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through this Weekend.\n\nA progressive weather pattern will continue through the week as weak \ndisturbances move through the mean flow. The first shortwave is \nexpected on Monday, then another around midweek. An uptick in winds \nare expected as this pieces of energy quickly move through but are \nnot expected to be impactful. Gusts will likely (80% chance or \ngreater) remain under 25 MPH outside of the Colorado River Valley \nwhere enhanced north winds are possible Wednesday into Thursday. \nOutside of the Sierra crest, it will remain dry as moisture will be \nlacking with these features. The latest forecast has decreased snow \npotential in the Sierra and even there it may struggle to get \naccumulating snow. \n\nTemperatures will start out below normal in most locations. As \nridging builds in between each system, temperatures will warm warm \nthrough the week. By the end of the work week, temperatures should \nclimb a few degrees above normal. Despite another quick system \nmoving through this weekend that could produce another uptick in \nwinds and low chances for precipitation, it should remain above \nnormal through the weekend. Low confidence with the weekend system \nand if it will produce wind and/or rain and snow impacts, however at \nthis time no significant impacts are expected as the system would be \nweak. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...\nWinds remain light and somewhat variable through the forecast \nperiod, but will generally follow diurnal patterns. Light SW winds \nunder 5 kt early this morning become NE by late morning, then ESE by \nmid afternoon and remaining under 8 kt. Increasing mid-level and \nhigh clouds are expected through the forecast period, with bases at \nor above 12-15kft. VFR conditions prevail.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light diurnal winds \nmainly under 8 kt will continue for the terminals through the \nforecast period. Terrain induced winds may reach 10 kt this morning \nand early afternoon at KIFP, otherwise no wind concerns around the \nregion. Mid-level and high clouds will be present into Monday \nmorning, though bases are expected to remain at or above 12-15kft. \nVFR conditions prevail.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson\nAVIATION...Moore\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
